<article class="env-lemma" id="0AX1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AX1">Lemma <span data-tag="0AX1">47.16.1</span>.</a> Let $(A, \mathfrak m, \kappa ) \to (B, \mathfrak m', \kappa ')$ be a finite local map of Noetherian local rings. Let $\omega _ A^\bullet $ be a normalized dualizing complex. Then $\omega _ B^\bullet = R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (B, \omega _ A^\bullet )$ is a normalized dualizing complex for $B$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/0AX0" data-tag="0AX0">47.15.8</a> the complex $\omega _ B^\bullet $ is dualizing for $B$. We have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ B(\kappa ', \omega _ B^\bullet ) = R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ B(\kappa ', R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (B, \omega _ A^\bullet )) = R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ A(\kappa ', \omega _ A^\bullet )  \]
</div>
    <p>
       by Lemma <a href="/tag/0A70" data-tag="0A70">47.13.1</a>. Since $\kappa '$ is isomorphic to a finite direct sum of copies of $\kappa $ as an $A$-module and since $\omega _ A^\bullet $ is normalized, we see that this complex only has cohomology placed in degree $0$. Thus $\omega _ B^\bullet $ is a normalized dualizing complex as well. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>